<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sangha">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sangha</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as saṅgha, saṃgha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sangha is a term meaning association, assembly or community. Historically it denoted the governing assembly of a republic or a kingdom, and it has long been used by religious associations, among them Buddhists, Jains, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> it refers above all to the monastic communities of monks (bhikkhu) and nuns (bhikkhuni), established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Gautama Buddha</a> in the fifth century BCE for those who wished to practise full-time, free of household life. Members follow the vinaya, whose 227 main rules include complete chastity and eating only before noon. The sangha is the third of the Three Refuges, and it has preserved, translated and spread the Buddha's teachings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sangha.jpg" alt="Sangha" width="330" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Darrenjsmith · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RatoMonks.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
